AB  - ABSTRACT While much is known about how the media covers mass shootings within the United States and how this coverage impacts the public, little is known about the consumption and perceptions of international mass shootings. To address this gap, this study examines the relationship between social media consumption and perceptions of mass shootings, both national and international. Specifically, using an online survey, the relationship between social media consumption and perceptions of mass shootings as a United States problem and/or a global problem are explored among a sample of college students (N = 407). Ordinal logistic regressions show that social media consumption, overall, is not found to influence the perception of mass shootings as a problem. However, respondents’ fear of violent crime and their political ideology mattered toward the perception of mass shootings as a problem after accounting for social media consumption.
