TI  - Linguistic Analysis of Disputed Meanings: Threats
AU  - Tammy Gales
AU  - Marlon Hurt
JO  - The Encyclopedia of Applied Linguistics
PY  - 2025
SP  - 1
EP  - 7
DO  - 10.1002/9781405198431.wbeal0711.pub2
AB  - Abstract Threats are made for a variety of reasons—for example, providing humor, attracting attention, venting anger, and instilling fear. Recipients of threats must weigh several factors in order to interpret which meaning may have been intended. A recipient may focus on the actual words used to suggest a threatening action (i.e., its locution); they may report feeling fear that the threat will be carried out (i.e., the perlocution); or they may focus on the perceived intention of the threatener (i.e., the illocution). When a communication is interpreted to be threatening and is reported to law enforcement, it undergoes a threat assessment, a process in which investigators examine several context‐dependent social, psychological, and linguistic factors associated with the threat to attempt to determine how likely it is that the threat will be carried out. This determination of threatener intent has been taken up differently by various communities, from behavioral practitioners and psychological scholars, who mostly focus on threatener form as an indicator of intent, to linguistic scholars, who highlight the function of the threatener's language. This article surveys recent practice and research in these three communities. The findings demonstrate how many of the language features in current threat assessment frameworks are unreliable as direct indicators of behavior but point to promising research from psychological and linguistic scholars that correlates language form, linguistic function, and threatener behavior. Given the immediate, real‐world implications for threat assessment practitioners, further research in this area is strongly encouraged to empirically validate and support this process.
