AB  - Mass murder is rare, but these attacks greatly impact society at large. There is the loss of the victims killed, the effect on the injured victims, and the impact on their families and the community. Mass murder encompasses the killing of multiple victims in a single attack. Within this typology, mass shootings and lone-wolf domestic terrorism will be the focus for this dissertation. The offenders of mass murder attacks such as these often commit them alone which provides unique challenges for detection and prevention. This dissertation accounts for demographic, situational, psychological, and motivational factors related to the lethality and harm of mass murder attacks, along with domestic terrorism and mass shooting incidents. Additionally, this dissertation allows for comparison between domestic terrorism and mass shooting attacks to discern if these attacks are different. Data was utilized from The Violence Project for mass shooting events and The Lone-Wolf Terrorism in America: Using Knowledge of Radicalization Pathways to Forge Prevention Strategies database (ICPSR 36107) for lone-wolf domestic terrorism attacks. There were 165 mass shootings and 98 domestic terrorist attacks. When the two datasets were combined to create a mass murder dataset, there were 242 cases; there were five cases that overlapped in the two datasets and the duplicates were dropped. Results indicated that various factors were salient in the mass murder analyses. Results indicated that firearms, government buildings or places, and ideological and triggering events were salient for the number of victims killed and injured in the domestic terrorism analyses. None of the variables in the mass shooting analyses were significant.
