AB  - A time sequence analysis is conducted on 125 lone-actor terrorists, most of whom mounted attacks in Europe and North America, utilizing the Terrorist Radicalization Assessment Protocol (TRAP-18), a structured professional judgment instrument with demonstrable interrater reliability and criterion, discriminant, and predictive validity. Both frequency ﬁ lters ( ≥ 3) and coef ﬁ cient ﬁ lters ( > .50) were applied to the data. Results indicate that virtually all distal characteristics, such as criminal violence, mental disorder, and ideology, preceded the proximal warning behaviors, such as pathway, ﬁ xation, identi ﬁ cation, leakage, last resort, and directly communicated threats. Indicators that were “ gatekeepers ” and “ turning point events ” were also identi ﬁ ed (Taylor et al., 2008). The time sequence analysis further validates the model of the TRAP-18 as a risk instrument for the assessment and management of lone-actor terrorist violence.
